Cryptographic Hashing and Secure Password Storage
Overview

This experiment focuses on implementing secure password storage techniques using cryptographic hashing algorithms. It compares weak hashing methods (MD5, SHA-256) with modern secure hashing techniques involving salting and password hashing functions.

Objectives
Understand cryptographic hashing concepts
Compare MD5, SHA-256, and secure password hashing
Implement password hashing with salt
Demonstrate secure password storage mechanism

Tools Used
Python / PHP
OpenSSL (optional)
Built-in hashing libraries (hashlib, password_hash)
VS Code

Methodology
Input a plain-text password
Generate hash using MD5 and SHA-256
Compare hash outputs
Implement salted hashing
Store and verify hashed passwords

Hashing Techniques Demonstrated
MD5 (Weak – deprecated for security use)
SHA-256 (Improved but still not sufficient alone)
Bcrypt / Password Hashing with Salt (Recommended)

Key Observations
MD5 is vulnerable to collision attacks
SHA-256 alone is not sufficient for password storage
Salting prevents rainbow table attacks
Bcrypt significantly increases brute-force resistance

Secure Implementation Example
Password hashing with salt
Secure verification instead of plain comparison

Outcome

The experiment demonstrated why modern systems avoid simple hashing and rely on salted, adaptive hashing algorithms for secure password storage.

Conclusion

Secure password storage requires strong hashing algorithms combined with salting. This ensures protection against brute-force and precomputed attacks.